Subject: hyper-transport says which suite and group a connection negotiated
Why: Every link of the joint kiln, Hex and Shards plan takes TLS13_AES_256_GCM_SHA384 alone, and a test at each end of a connection asserts the suite it negotiated (Ada, 2026-11-11: "all such communications should be AESGCM256 bidirectional encrypted"); hyper-transport holds each connection's TLS session and gave no way to read it. Endpoint::negotiated reads it from hyper-quic's handshake data, as kiln's reach links read theirs from rustls. Hex's own change (its vendor/patches/hyper-raft/0002), which hex-quic calls: kiln holds it so Hex builds on kiln's one hyper-raft.

diff --git a/crates/hyper-transport/src/endpoint.rs b/crates/hyper-transport/src/endpoint.rs
index 0b3d6d8507b01a352e2fe19f93ff8c46f292a148..9760487f2c1222be65990fa569b66b57a2bd6466 100644
--- a/crates/hyper-transport/src/endpoint.rs
+++ b/crates/hyper-transport/src/endpoint.rs
@@ -1787,6 +1787,29 @@ impl<C: Classes, B: Budget<C::Class>, D: Directory<Role = C::Role>> Endpoint<C,
         Ok(conn.epoch)
     }
 
+    /// The TLS 1.3 cipher suite and key exchange group the connection to `peer` negotiated, once
+    /// its handshake has chosen them: what an owner whose links take one suite alone reports and
+    /// asserts, at each end of a connection. `None` without a connection to `peer`, or before its
+    /// handshake has chosen a suite.
+    pub fn negotiated(
+        &self,
+        peer: PeerId,
+    ) -> Option<(
+        hyper_quic::rustls::CipherSuite,
+        Option<hyper_quic::rustls::NamedGroup>,
+    )> {
+        let key = self.core.connection_of(peer)?;
+        let conn = self.conns.get(key)?.as_ref()?;
+        let data = conn.quic.crypto_session().handshake_data()?;
+        let data = data
+            .downcast::<hyper_quic::crypto::rustls::HandshakeData>()
+            .ok()?;
+        Some((
+            data.negotiated_cipher_suite?,
+            data.negotiated_key_exchange_group,
+        ))
+    }
+
